<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarization requires.

  Deliberately short. Two things are NOT here and their absence is the point:

  * No app sandbox. A sandboxed process cannot read another volume's files or
    mount a filesystem, which is the entire function of this application. Adding
    com.apple.security.app-sandbox would leave it able to do nothing at all.

  * No com.apple.security.automation.apple-events. The privileged mount runs by
    exec'ing /usr/bin/osascript as a subprocess, so this process never sends an
    Apple event itself. If that ever moves in-process — NSAppleScript instead of
    exec — this entitlement becomes necessary and its absence will present as the
    prompt silently never appearing.

  Full Disk Access is not an entitlement and cannot be requested here. It is a TCC
  grant the user gives in System Settings, checked against the identity of the
  binary making the call, which is why the privileged half re-execs this
  application rather than elevating /sbin/mount_apfs.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  JavaScriptCore compiles JavaScript at run time, and the hardened runtime
	  forbids that by default. WKWebView usually runs its content out of process
	  with its own entitlements, so this may prove unnecessary — but the failure
	  mode without it is a window that loads and then does nothing, which is far
	  more expensive to diagnose than one permissive flag on a non-sandboxed
	  application already trusted with the whole disk.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
